{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DX7xaZc7k7UWVxKPVvpXLn","type":"tool_result","content":"<persisted-output>\nOutput too large (32.8KB). Full output saved to: /ard-home/.claude/projects/-Users-al--ard-work-albertcbrown-ard-01M8FC5TB3MWYREWQ39DWNHZS7/68390275-e081-4aad-9057-42f5ad4a9a58/tool-results/bx8zrid4m.txt\n\nPreview (first 2KB):\n(eval):1: no matches found: --include=*.go\ninternal/scanner/finding.go:93:func DecodeFindings(r io.Reader) ([]Finding, error) {\ninternal/scanner/llm.go:19:func ParseLLMFingerprint(fp string) (rule, p, symbol string, ok bool) {\ninternal/scanner/llm.go:53:func CheckLLMFingerprints(fs []Finding, exists func(path string) bool) DecodeErrors {\n47:**Stack (decided, #9).** Go, one static binary (CGO off) with: cobra, gopkg.in/yaml.v3, modernc.org/sqlite, go-github v88 with ghinstallation/v2, log/slog, golang.org/x/tools (for the architecture test only) and an internal `internal/gitx` for git.\n74:| `ard:pending` | Scanner or human | Ready for triage; the lead's request label. On an LLM-scanner issue (route `none`) only a human sets it, and it must be `ard:pending` itself even when intake uses a custom label, because the intake sweep skips App-authored issues. Also set by the engineer's close-out in a network round, to re-run the lead |\n192:A scanner is a directory, `.ard/scanners/<name>/`, holding either an executable `scan` that prints findings as JSON lines or a `prompt.txt` for an LLM scanner. The controller does all dedupe, grouping, filing and closing, so deterministic tools and LLM scanners share one pipeline and no scanner holds a GitHub token.\n...\n</persisted-output>","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7b0723cd-7468-4fa1-8529-c21679c5044c","uuid":"ba806934-9650-4b12-a991-6f12aa00f3f5","timestamp":"2026-11-27T20:47:13.914Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"(eval):1: no matches found: --include=*.go\ninternal/scanner/finding.go:93:func DecodeFindings(r io.Reader) ([]Finding, error) {\ninternal/scanner/llm.go:19:func ParseLLMFingerprint(fp string) (rule, p, symbol string, ok bool) {\ninternal/scanner/llm.go:53:func CheckLLMFingerprints(fs []Finding, exists func(path string) bool) DecodeErrors {\n47:**Stack (decided, #9).** Go, one static binary (CGO off) with: cobra, gopkg.in/yaml.v3, modernc.org/sqlite, go-github v88 with ghinstallation/v2, log/slog, golang.org/x/tools (for the architecture test only) and an internal `internal/gitx` for git.\n74:| `ard:pending` | Scanner or human | Ready for triage; the lead's request label. On an LLM-scanner issue (route `none`) only a human sets it, and it must be `ard:pending` itself even when intake uses a custom label, because the intake sweep skips App-authored issues. Also set by the engineer's close-out in a network round, to re-run the lead |\n192:A scanner is a directory, `.ard/scanners/<name>/`, holding either an executable `scan` that prints findings as JSON lines or a `prompt.txt` for an LLM scanner. The controller does all dedupe, grouping, filing and closing, so deterministic tools and LLM scanners share one pipeline and no scanner holds a GitHub token.\n205:**LLM scanners (`prompt.txt`) file under extra rules.** `autoclose: true` on one is a config error: an LLM scanner never closes an issue for absence. `every` defaults to at least 24h and a value below 24h is an error; `max_budget_usd`, when set, must be above 0. An LLM fingerprint is `rule:path` or `rule:path:symbol` (rule `[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]{0,63}`, a clean relative path without `..` that is also listed in the finding's `files` and exists, symbol `[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_./-]{0,127}`); a bad fingerprint is reported by finding index only, never by content. Before grouping, a finding whose title is a near-duplicate of an open issue's title or checklist line, or of a more severe finding in the same run, is skipped without a ledger row, so it is offered again next run. Titles are lowercased, reduced to `[a-z0-9]` tokens, minus one-character tokens and a short stop list; they match when the token sets are equal, or when both have at least three tokens and their Jaccard similarity is at least 0.8. An issue filed by a scanner whose route is not `pending` ends with a footer explaining that a human adds `ard:pending` to send it to triage or closes it as not planned to dismiss it. The stale closer also considers an open issue whose only `ard:` label is its scanner label (route `none`). `ard serve` runs them through the LLM engine below.\n207:**The LLM engine** (`scanner.AgentEngine`, #204) runs the role's agent as a `scanner`-stage job (Issue 0) on a read-only worktree at the scanned commit, with the role's sidecar, a writable output dir (`$ARD_OUT`) and the model credential only; the whole repository and the sidecar are untrusted input. With no model credential it asks for no slot and runs nothing. The agent writes its findings to `$ARD_OUT/findings.jsonl`, which must be a regular file (never a symlink) of at most 16 MiB; it is decoded with the strict schema above, then checked against the fingerprint grammar, and a missing file is a failed run. Usage is recorded before any outcome check, so a failed run keeps its cost. A usage-limit stop is recorded in the limits book and the job ends as limited; if the book cannot record it, the run fails instead. A timeout, a vanished process, a non-zero exit, an unsuccessful result or an invalid findings file is a failed scan whose error carries the exit code and result subtype, or line numbers, and never agent text. The engine does no redaction itself: its decoded findings pass the same pre-filing guard as every scanner's (below), so a secret hidden behind a JSON escape in the file is still caught, and only after the guard passes are `@`-mentions in titles and bodies defused (`closeout.Sanitize`: a zero-width space after `@`, comment openers escaped), so an inserted character can never split a secret pattern the guard should see.\n209:**Stops that are not runs.** An LLM scanner skipped because no model credential is set (`scanner.skip reason=no_model_credential`; `ard serve` then builds no agent engine, as in the spark pod's `--loops scanners,...`), skipped because the executor cannot run it (`executor: k8s` until #454: `reason=executor_unsupported`), or stopped by a usage limit (outcome `dropped_limited`, log `scanner.limited`) is not recorded: the scanner stays due, and the loop moves on to the next due scanner, so it never blocks a due `cve`. The loop checks the engine before it reads the scanner's state or GitHub, and warns once per scanner and reason, then logs at debug. A run interrupted by a controller restart does count: at startup the stale scanner job is failed and a `scan_failed` run is recorded from the job's creation time, so an LLM scanner does not spend again at once (deterministic scanners get the same record). Known leaks, documented rather than fixed: a pause mid-scan spends and files nothing; `--dry-run` spends real tokens, because the executor is not an effect (the spark dry-run pod has no credential, so it skips); an interrupted run's partial usage is not parsed at restart; scanner spend counts toward the daily caps (stage `scanner`) but has no row in any issue's cost table; and one LLM scan holds the scanners loop for up to its timeout, so `cve` waits behind it.\n211:**Route.** The scanner key `route` decides where its issues go: `pending` (default for executable scanners) files with `ard:pending`, so they enter triage; `none` (default for LLM scanners) files with the scanner label only, and a human adds `ard:pending` to send one issue to triage; `needs-human` files with `ard:needs-human` and no `ard:pending`, so no agent triages them. LLM scanners may not use `pending`. Changing `route` affects only new issues. A human promotes one issue by removing `ard:needs-human` and/or adding `ard:pending`; after that ard never joins findings onto it.\n217:3. **Scan.** Run the scanner on a read-only worktree of `origin/main`, with a timeout. It needs a free job slot, and scanners rank last for one. **A run that is denied a slot is not dropped: it is not recorded, stays due and is offered again on the next tick** (every five minutes), until it gets a slot or the controller stops. A failed scan (non-zero exit, timeout, invalid output, output the pre-filing guard flags) is recorded and changes nothing else. A run whose engine cannot start (no model credential, an executor without LLM-scanner support) or that a usage limit stops is not recorded and stays due, like a denied slot.\n222:**A scanner loop runs at most one due scanner per tick.** The `scanners` loop polls every five minutes, picks the most overdue scanner (last run plus its `every`; a dropped run counts as a run) and runs it. Pausing stops it, because it files and closes issues. A run denied a slot, skipped by its engine or stopped by a usage limit does not end the tick: the next due scanner is tried. Its tick timeout is the longest scan timeout of any scanner, LLM scanners included, plus 15 minutes, not the poll interval.\n226:`ard rebuild-state <scanner>` takes the leader lock (it refuses to run while `ard serve` holds it), reads every issue with `ard:scanner:<name>`, open and closed, and replaces the scanner's findings and suppressions in one transaction, keeping the run history. For each fingerprint, an open issue wins (the lowest number), else the highest-numbered closed issue decides: completed leaves a `fixed` tombstone, not planned or duplicate leaves none. Independently, the highest-numbered not-planned or duplicate close gives a suppression until `closed_at + stale.suppress_for`, kept even when expired. It prints what it adds, removes and changes; with `--dry-run` it prints and writes nothing. The dry-run state database holds synthetic issues that are not on GitHub, so it is never rebuilt. Any configured scanner can be rebuilt, LLM scanners included, since they file the same markers; the built-in stale closer keeps no rebuildable state. One known gap: reopening an old issue that shares a fingerprint with a newer one can leave the database different from a rebuild until the next rebuild-state.\n232:**LLM scanners need stable fingerprints.** Their prose changes run to run, so they fingerprint on rule, file and symbol, never on wording. Before filing, the controller also compares the title against the scanner's open issues and skips near-duplicates.\n246:| docs-drift | LLM: docs that contradict the code (planned; needs the LLM scanner engine, #204) | 3 d | 10 per doc file | no |\n247:| architecture | LLM against ADRs and the sidecar spec repo | 3 d | 1 | no |\n313:Local-executor details: the role directory (and its `Dockerfile`) is mounted from the config snapshot at the job's SHA, not from the working tree. The job worktree is always detached at a SHA resolved from the job's ref (the executor never checks out a branch; the committer owns branch worktrees). The controller's clone is a bare repository ard owns at `~/.ard/repos/<owner>/<repo>.git` (lowercased), with no remote, no stored credential and auto-gc off (`gc.auto=0`, `maintenance.auto=false`). `ard serve` creates it on first start and seeds it with one fetch of `main` (up to 30 min; if that fails, serve starts paused and the next start retries), and later starts reuse it. Config loads and committer lanes fetch into it, and every committer and executor worktree is cut from it (lanes under `~/.ard/work/<owner>/<repo>/lanes/`). `ard serve` never writes to the operator's checkout: it reads it only for the `origin` fallback of `--repo` and the local `paused` key. To rebuild a damaged clone, stop serve, `rm -rf` it and restart. Upgrading from a version that used the checkout: ard deletes its old lane and job worktree directories without running git there, so the old `refs/ard/*` refs and the dangling worktree registrations stay in the checkout until you remove them (`git for-each-ref --format='delete %(refname)' refs/ard/ | git update-ref --stdin; git worktree prune`). Every `git worktree add`, `remove` and `prune` on the controller's clone, by the executor and the committer's lanes alike, takes one in-process lock keyed by the clone's common git directory, exclusively, because git's worktree bookkeeping is not safe under concurrent admin commands and a fetch aborts if it reads a worktree entry that is half added or removed (#411); fetches take the same lock shared (they still run in parallel), and commits and pushes take neither. Each such command also has a 30 s deadline, and cleanup runs even when the job's context is cancelled, so one hung git cannot stall every lane. Do not run `git worktree prune` (or add/remove) in `~/.ard/repos/<owner>/<repo>.git` by hand while `ard serve` runs: the lock is per process and cannot cover a human's git. The operator's own checkout is free to use. The host keeps two files per job: `<base>.log` (stdout and stderr combined, the heartbeat source and the committed log) and `<base>.stdout` (stdout only, parsed by the adapter). A read-only job (`ReadOnly`, used by scanners) gets the worktree mounted `:ro` under Docker, or `chmod -R a-w` on the host. A scanner job may also ask for an output directory (`Job.Output`, scanner stage only; any other stage is `ErrInvalidJob`): `Prepare` creates an empty `<job home>/out` (0700), the job sees it at `$ARD_OUT` (`Handle.View.Out`; the host path in host mode, a writable bind mount at `/out` under Docker while the worktree stays `:ro`), and it is removed with the job home. It is how an LLM scanner hands back its findings file (#204); the Kubernetes executor refuses an `Output` job until its pod has such a mount (#454), so `ard serve` under `executor: k8s` skips LLM scanners without recording a run (`scanner.skip reason=executor_unsupported`) instead of failing each one at prepare. A scanner agent job is bounded by the executor's hard timeout and the role's `max_budget_usd`; the Reaper leaves scanner-stage jobs to the scanner tick. The heartbeat is the log file's mtime while the PID is alive; liveness alone is not a beat. Phase 0 local jobs do not survive a controller restart: the pipes belong to the controller, so after a restart the job reports `orphaned` (PID alive, start time verified) or `gone`, and the Reaper kills and fails it. The executor is not an effect: it never calls GitHub or commits, so jobs run normally under `--dry-run`. Sidecar auth: the sidecar fetch runs in the controller with the GitHub App installation token in a host-scoped `http.<url>.extraheader` passed through git's environment, never argv; the job receives plain files and no credential, and the cache stores no remote and no token. The App must be installed on the sidecar repo with contents:read. Under `executor: k8s` the `pre` container instead gets a per-job installation token scoped to contents:read on the job's repositories (`github.AppAuth.MintReadToken`), revoked when the job ends; a personal access token cannot be scoped down, so that executor needs GitHub App auth. Host-mode jobs can reach `~/.ard/sidecars`, but it holds no credential. Host-mode jobs run as the controller's uid: they can read the GitHub App key and `~/.config/gh` by absolute path, write the controller's clone, and undo `chmod a-w`. Host mode is allowed for every role ([#84](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/84)), but a role that reads untrusted input (every triage, worker and reviewer role, and an LLM scanner) warns loudly when it has no `Dockerfile`; deterministic scanners are exempt, and nothing warns under `executor: k8s`, where every job runs in a container. `ard config check` and the first successful config load of `ard serve` (and any role a later config adds) log one `executor.host_untrusted` WARN per such role and also write it to stderr, so it survives `--log-level`. The local executor logs the WARN again for every host-mode job of such a role and appends a `host_untrusted` row to the `events` table. **Default agent image under the local executor (#244, part of #55).** `ard serve` and `ard config check` read the host setting `ARD_AGENT_IMAGE`: a digest-pinned reference (`<name>@sha256:<64 hex>`) or a local image ID (`sha256:<hex>` from `make agent-image`); tags are refused (`executor.CheckImageRef`, `ErrImageRef`), and an invalid value stops startup. When it is set, `Prepare` runs every job that reads untrusted input and has no `Dockerfile` (triage, worker agent, test and fix steps, reviewer, and LLM scanner jobs) under Docker in that image, with the same flags and mounts as a Dockerfile role (`--cap-drop ALL`, `no-new-privileges`, worktree at `/work`, role directory `:ro`, and so on). A role's own `Dockerfile` wins. Deterministic scanners (trusted input) stay on the host. A local image is used as is; a digest that is not present is pulled. There is no fallback to host mode: `ard serve` runs `docker version` at startup and fails if Docker is down, and a job whose Docker setup fails at `Prepare` fails there. The controller's clone is not mounted, so `git` has no repository inside the container; every Docker job gets `GOFLAGS=-buildvcs=false` so `go build` does not fail on VCS stamping. Unset keeps today's behaviour. With it set, `ard config check` and `ard serve` no longer log or record `executor.host_untrusted` for roles it covers, and the hint of the warning names `ARD_AGENT_IMAGE`. Each warning lists what the job can reach, including `~/.ard` (state, config snapshots, the pause file, other jobs' worktrees and logs). The owner accepts this risk while ard runs only on owner-controlled repos, and revisits it when moving to the spark cluster, where every job runs in a container once the k8s executor (#54) lands. Until then the Gate 1 dry-run soak on spark (see Rollout and Delivery) runs `ard serve` with the local executor in a pod, and the deterministic cve scanner runs there as a host subprocess of the controller container: its role directory has no `Dockerfile`, and the `ard` image carries its pinned tools. Only deterministic scanners run in that pod: it starts `ard serve --loops scanners,stale,reaper` ([#187](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/187)), so the triage, worker and reviewer loops never run there, even though `.ard/config.yaml` on `main` has agent roles. `--loops` takes a comma-separated list of loop names (scanners, stale, triage, workers, reviewers, reviewfix, merger, janitor, reaper, budget; `budget` is the status-issue notice loop, the claim gate is always on), defaults to every loop, and rejects an unknown name with exit 2. `ard serve` and `ard config check` refuse to run on git older than 2.47.0, because the committer's fetches fail on older versions.\n336:**Agent CLIs plug in through adapters.** Each adapter turns (prompt file, context paths, budget) into a command line and parses the exit code and, where the CLI reports them, turns and cost. For Claude Code that is `claude -p --output-format=stream-json --verbose --restricted --safe-mode --strict-mcp-config --disable-slash-commands --permission-mode=dontAsk --settings='{\"includeGitInstructions\":false,\"disableAllHooks\":true}' --tools=… --allowed-tools=… --disallowed-tools=… --model <model> --max-budget-usd <max_budget_usd> --append-system-prompt-file <role prompt> -- <message>` (`--verbose` is required by `stream-json` in print mode). Every stage (scanner, triage, worker, review) gets the same tool profile: `--tools` and `--allowed-tools` are `Read,Glob,Grep,Edit,Write,Bash`, and `--disallowed-tools` is `Bash(git commit *),Bash(git push *),Bash(git remote *),Bash(gh *)` (decided, #79). ard does not pass `--bare`: it limits the CLI to Bash, Read and Edit, and `--tools` cannot widen it (#105). The planted-config isolation `--bare` gave comes instead from `--restricted` (only managed settings and `--settings` load), `--safe-mode` (no CLAUDE.md or AGENTS.md, skills, agents, hooks, MCP servers or auto memory), `--strict-mcp-config`, `--disable-slash-commands`, and `--settings` that turns off hooks and the git status snapshot (commit subjects are third-party text). `CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_BACKGROUND_TASKS=1` is set as a fixed env var (the executor allows it) so Bash cannot detach commands. The integration tests pin each of these against a worktree with planted config and must be re-run on every `TestedVersion` bump. The tool list is not a security boundary, and the deny list is advisory, because shell indirection gets around it. The real boundaries are the missing GitHub token (Docker; see the host-mode note above, #84), the committer, which commits only `.ard/<issue#>/` from a triage or review job (other edits are discarded with the worktree), and the diff guard on worker changes. An explicit tool list from the caller (`Spec.Tools`) replaces the allow list and keeps the deny list. The user message holds only fixed text and the paths of the untrusted input files. The scanner stage (an LLM scanner, #204) has no issue (`Spec.Issue` is 0) and no untrusted file list: its message says that everything it can read, the repository in the working directory and any sidecar dir, is untrusted data, and asks for findings as JSON lines in `Spec.OutputFile` (`$ARD_OUT/findings.jsonl`), which is required for that stage and refused for every other. Because the CLI's Write and Edit tools reach only the working directory and the `--add-dir` dirs, and a scanner's worktree is read-only, the adapter adds `--add-dir=<dir of OutputFile>` after the read dirs; an integration test pins that a Write elsewhere fails or is denied. Bash is not confined by it: the scanner stage keeps the standard tool profile, Bash included. The installed CLI accepts an undocumented `--max-turns`; ard does not rely on it, so a per-job dollar cap and the executor's hard timeout bound each run. Agents run with an empty per-job CLI config dir and HOME and receive exactly one model credential, passed by name by the executor and never visible to the adapter: an API key (`ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`) or a subscription OAuth token (`CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN`, from `claude setup-token`); that rests on the environment, not on a CLI flag. `ard serve` chooses the credential from its own environment: `ARD_AGENT_AUTH` is `auto` (the default; the token if set, else the key), `oauth` or `apikey`, and an explicit mode never falls back to the other variable. Only one is ever passed, because the CLI prefers the API key and would silently bill it (pinned by an integration test). A missing credential does not stop startup, because deterministic scanners need none: `ard serve` logs one INFO line, and a job that needs one fails with a clear error. The executor creates the per-job config dir as `<job home>/.claude` (0700) in `prepare` (`Handle.View.ConfigDir`; under Docker it is a bind mount at `/ard-config`), refuses to start a job whose config dir is not that empty directory, and removes it with the job home; the Reaper's `KeepWorktree` retention keeps it too, and it holds no credential, since the credential travels only in the environment. `CLAUDE_CODE_SUBPROCESS_ENV_SCRUB=1`, which would keep the token out of Bash children, is not set (decided, #125). Residual risk: an API key (`ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`) is inherited by Bash children; on the tested release the CLI does not pass `CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN` to them; and either credential can still be read from the `claude` process's own environment (`/proc/$PPID/environ` on Linux, `ps eww` on macOS). The container is the boundary: it holds no GitHub token, and the credential is scoped to the model. The scrub is off because on 2.1.328 it requires bubblewrap (and socat) plus unprivileged user namespaces; Docker blocks those under ard's flags (`--cap-drop ALL`, `no-new-privileges`, default seccomp), so every Bash call would fail closed and without bwrap the CLI refuses to start. On macOS host mode it worked and matched the `dontAsk` outcomes. It can be revisited for host mode or a loosened container. What agent Bash can read differs by mode: under Docker, only the job's own credential; in host mode, far more, because any same-uid process's environment is readable (`ps eww <pid>` on macOS, `/proc/<pid>/environ` on Linux), including `ard serve`'s own, which holds both credentials. The scrub would not close that; it is part of the host-mode risk of #84. `kimi` and `dsh-tui` both run headless with `-p`; neither has a turn cap and `kimi` reports no cost, so turns and cost are optional in the adapter result. `config.yaml` names the default CLI and any per-role override, so a role can move between CLIs without touching its prompt. The Claude adapter also reads a usage limit and token usage from the stream: a limit comes from a `rate_limit_event` with status `rejected` (its `resetsAt` is the reset time), from the CLI's own error text (`You've hit your session, weekly, Opus or Sonnet limit`) and from `rate_limit` or `billing_error` errors, and it records `usage` and `modelUsage` token counts (by input, output, cache write and cache read; per model when the result has `modelUsage`, which is preferred for accounting). That text is matched only on paths the CLI itself writes (an assistant message carrying an `error`, or an errored result with HTTP 429 or `api_error`), never in agent output, because an agent can be made to print the phrase. Only the job's own agent step and its fix steps are read for a limit: a worker's test step runs agent-written code, so its output is never classified (a printed limit line there cannot block the account). With an API key a 429 stays a transient `rate_limit` retry; only subscription (OAuth) runs have usage limits. The adapter also reads `rate_limit_info.unifiedWindows` from every `rate_limit_event` into `Result.Windows` (one reading per window type, the last winning, also when the run ends with no result). The controller records the readings for OAuth steps only, from the job's own agent and fix steps and never from a test step, including killed steps with no result; it logs them as `limits.reading`. A recording error is logged as `limits.observe_failed` and never fails the job. Agent jobs are gated on the readings (#63; `limits:` below): at 75% of the 5-hour window (`stop_workers_pct`) no new worker, triage or agent scanner starts and at most one `agent: claude` job runs at a time (the pool's `DeniedSerial` verdict, 5-hour window only); at 90% (`stop_all_pct`) no new agent job starts. The weekly window is paced by elapsed time: `weekly_stop_workers_pct_by_day` has one percent for each of the 7 days of the window, counted from `resets_at` minus 7 days, and on day N new workers, triage and agent scanners stop once weekly usage reaches the N-th value (with no `resets_at`, the last value applies); `weekly_stop_all_pct` is flat. Reviewers may start until the stop-all values. A reading whose reset time has passed is ignored. An Opus limit (the reactive pause) blocks only Opus roles. Deterministic scanners, the merger and roles of other agents are never gated. The committed fixtures are scrubbed real captures where a human has made them (`testdata/CAPTURE.md`) and clearly named `synthetic_*` files elsewhere.\n518:Scanners also take `route` (`pending`, `none` or `needs-human`). Anything unset takes its default: intake in `label` mode with `ard:pending`, scanners every 4 h with `max_open` 5 and `group_size` 10, triage every 15 min, a 30-minute job timeout, `make test`, 2 test-fix attempts, 3 review rounds and worker diff thresholds of 2,000 lines (warn) and 3,000 lines (hold). Durations accept `m`, `h` and `d`. A sidecar `repo` must be `owner/name` and its `ref` a valid git ref name (no `:`, leading `-`, `..` or spaces; an abbreviated SHA is rejected, use all 40 characters); `ard config check` reports the file and key. A worker's `timeout` and `max_budget_usd` cover the whole job (every step), and `tests: \"\"` disables its test step. Per-role keys are `every`, `max_open`, `group_size`, `autoclose` (only for a scanner with a `scan` executable; `true` on an LLM scanner is a config error), `timeout`, `agent`, `model`, `max_budget_usd`, `budget_usd_per_day` (that role's own daily cap in USD; unset or 0 means only the repo cap applies), `sidecar`, `after`, `plan_section`, `tests`, `max_test_fixes`, `max_review_rounds`, `diff_warn_lines` and `diff_max_lines` (worker roles; positive integers, warn no more than max; above warn the PR proceeds with a note, above max it is held), `reviewers` and `when` (`paths`, or `triage` roles that ran; at least one is required). `ard config check` warns when every reviewer has a `when:` rule, because a PR matching none gets no review and stays a draft. `plan_section` is for triage roles: the `## <heading>` of `plan.md` the role owns (one line, no leading `#`, unique across triage roles). The starter roles default to Requirements (product-manager), Design (architect), UX (designer), Docs (documentation-planner) and Implementation (engineer), and a value in `config.yaml` overrides that; a triage role with none may write only its own role file. There are no per-role config files: `ard init` writes a role's starting overrides into `config.yaml` for the roles it ships. Once a second repo adopts ard, role directories can move to a shared roles repo pinned by ref, like a sidecar; a repo's own directory of the same name wins. `after` names triage roles, with no self-reference and no cycles.\n552:- **LLM scanners are the one timer-driven agent role.** Every other agent job starts from an admitted issue or PR; an LLM scanner reads all of `main` plus its sidecar on a schedule, so any merged text can try to steer it, and in host mode it runs as the controller's user with the standard tool profile, Bash included (the #84 host-mode risk, warning only). What bounds it: one model credential and no GitHub token; its only output is `$ARD_OUT/findings.jsonl`, strictly decoded and fingerprint-checked; the pre-filing guard fails closed on the decoded values before anything reaches an issue or `issue.md`, and mentions are defused after it; its issues are filed without `ard:pending` (`route: none`), so a human decides before any triage, worker or merger spend; each run has the role's `max_budget_usd` (required) and `timeout`, `every` is at least 24h, the daily caps count its jobs, and usage is recorded for failed and limited runs too.\n593:- **Agent image ([#320](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/320)).** The default `ard-agent` image (`deploy/agent/Dockerfile`) is the agent container of every Kubernetes job pod and the local executor's `ARD_AGENT_IMAGE`. It holds the `claude` CLI pinned to `claude.TestedVersion` with per-architecture checksums, git, make, the Go toolchain and the starter roles' tools at the scanner Dockerfiles' pins (jq, yq, osv-scanner, govulncheck, golangci-lint) plus shellcheck; no `gh`, no `ard` (the `pre` container copies a static one into `/ard-bin`), no credential helper, no AGPL tools (no trufflehog). It is built on the pinned `GO_IMAGE` like the `Dockerfile`, with no `RUN` on the target platform, and runs as 65532:65532 (`jobpod.AgentUID`) on a read-only root with `DISABLE_AUTOUPDATER=1`. It is **public** (owner decision on #320, which also covers redistributing the `claude` binary): `ard-build` builds it for `linux/amd64` and `linux/arm64` from the same commit as the ard image and pushes `ghcr.io/albertcbrown/ard/agent` by digest. Because both images are public they must hold no secrets: `make image-test` builds both and scans each exported filesystem (`internal/imagescan`, run by `deploy/imagescan`), failing on any credential-shaped file name (SSH keys, `.netrc`, `.git-credentials`, docker, kube, gh or claude credentials) or content (the redact package's token rules and PEM private keys with a body); the only exemptions are public test fixtures (Go's own testdata, GnuTLS self-test vectors), and a canary image wi","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"persistedOutputPath":"/ard-home/.claude/projects/-Users-al--ard-work-albertcbrown-ard-01M8FC5TB3MWYREWQ39DWNHZS7/68390275-e081-4aad-9057-42f5ad4a9a58/tool-results/bx8zrid4m.txt","persistedOutputSize":33564},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01DX7xaZc7k7UWVxKPVvpXLn","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
