{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01YDBRj18oMfq85eX72cybZD","type":"tool_result","content":"\t\treturn 5\n\tcase SevHigh:\n\t\treturn 4\n\tcase SevMedium:\n\t\treturn 3\n\tcase SevLow:\n\t\treturn 2\n\tcase SevInfo:\n\t\treturn 1\n\t}\n\treturn 0\n}\n\n// Valid reports whether s is one of the five severities.\nfunc (s Severity) Valid() bool { return s.Rank() > 0 }\n\n// Limits on scanner output. Any violation fails the whole run.\nconst (\n\tMaxLineBytes   = 1 << 20  // one JSONL line\n\tMaxStreamBytes = 16 << 20 // the whole stdout\n\tMaxFindings    = 10000\n\tMaxBodyBytes   = 64 << 10\n\tMaxFingerprint = 512 // bytes\n\tMaxTitleRunes  = 200\n\tMaxGroupBytes  = 256\n\tMaxFiles       = 1000\n\tmaxPathBytes   = 4096\n\tmaxErrorLines  = 20\n)\n\n// Finding is one line of scanner output.\ntype Finding struct {\n\tFingerprint string   `json:\"fingerprint\"` // required; stable across runs\n\tTitle       string   `json:\"title\"`       // required; one line\n\tSeverity    Severity `json:\"severity\"`    // required\n\tGroup       string   `json:\"group\"`       // optional; \"\" = one issue per finding\n\tFiles       []string `json:\"files\"`       // optional; repo-relative\n\tBody        string   `json:\"body\"`        // optional markdown\n}\n\n// DecodeError is one invalid line of scanner output.\ntype DecodeError struct {\n\tLine int\n\tMsg  string\n}\n\nfunc (e DecodeError) Error() string { return fmt.Sprintf(\"line %d: %s\", e.Line, e.Msg) }\n\n// DecodeErrors lists the invalid lines of a scanner's output, at most 20.\ntype DecodeErrors []DecodeError\n\nfunc (e DecodeErrors) Error() string {\n\tparts := make([]string, 0, len(e))\n\tfor _, d := range e {\n\t\tparts = append(parts, d.Error())\n\t}\n\treturn \"invalid scanner output: \" + strings.Join(parts, \"; \")\n}\n\n// DecodeFindings parses scanner stdout as JSON lines, strictly: unknown keys,\n// a missing required key, a bad value, a duplicate fingerprint or an\n// oversized line or stream are errors. Blank lines are skipped. It never\n// returns partial findings, so a half-parsed run can never trigger autoclose.\npackage scanner\n\nimport (\n\t\"fmt\"\n\t\"path\"\n\t\"regexp\"\n\t\"strings\"\n)\n\nvar (\n\tllmRuleRe   = regexp.MustCompile(`^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]{0,63}$`)\n\tllmPathRe   = regexp.MustCompile(`^[A-Za-z0-9._/@+-]+$`)\n\tllmSymbolRe = regexp.MustCompile(`^[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_./-]{0,127}$`)\n)\n\n// ParseLLMFingerprint splits an LLM scanner fingerprint, \"rule:path\" or\n// \"rule:path:symbol\", and reports whether it is well formed. The path must\n// be a clean, relative, repo-style path without \"..\" segments.\nfunc ParseLLMFingerprint(fp string) (rule, p, symbol string, ok bool) {\n\tif len(fp) > MaxFingerprint {\n\t\treturn \"\", \"\", \"\", false\n\t}\n\tparts := strings.Split(fp, \":\")\n\tif len(parts) < 2 || len(parts) > 3 {\n\t\treturn \"\", \"\", \"\", false\n\t}\n\trule, p = parts[0], parts[1]\n\tif len(parts) == 3 {\n\t\tsymbol = parts[2]\n\t\tif !llmSymbolRe.MatchString(symbol) {\n\t\t\treturn \"\", \"\", \"\", false\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tif !llmRuleRe.MatchString(rule) || !llmPathRe.MatchString(p) {\n\t\treturn \"\", \"\", \"\", false\n\t}\n\tif p == \".\" || strings.HasPrefix(p, \"/\") || path.Clean(p) != p {\n\t\treturn \"\", \"\", \"\", false\n\t}\n\tfor _, seg := range strings.Split(p, \"/\") {\n\t\tif seg == \"..\" {\n\t\t\treturn \"\", \"\", \"\", false\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn rule, p, symbol, true\n}\n\n// CheckLLMFingerprints validates every finding's fingerprint against the LLM\n// grammar, requires its path to be listed in the finding's Files and, via\n// exists, to be present in the repository. A nil exists treats every path as\n// present. Errors carry the 1-based finding index and fixed text only, never\n// the fingerprint or any finding text; at most 20 are returned.\nfunc CheckLLMFingerprints(fs []Finding, exists func(path string) bool) DecodeErrors {\n\tvar errs DecodeErrors\n\tfail := func(i int, msg string) {\n\t\tif len(errs) < maxErrorLines {\n\t\t\terrs = append(errs, DecodeError{Line: i + 1, Msg: msg})\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tfor i, f := range fs {\n\t\t_, p, _, ok := ParseLLMFingerprint(f.Fingerprint)\n\t\tif !ok {\n\t\t\tfail(i, \"fingerprint is not rule:path[:symbol]\")\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\tlisted := false\n\t\tfor _, file := range f.Files {\n\t\t\tif file == p {\n\t\t\t\tlisted = true\n\t\t\t\tbreak\n| Scanner | Engine | Every | Group size | Auto-close |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| cve | osv-scanner and govulncheck (trivy and the Dependabot alerts API later) | 6 h | 1 | yes |\n| lts | endoflife.date v1 API + manifests: go.mod, package.json, Dockerfile `FROM`, Helm `values.yaml` images | 7 d | 1 per component (product and cycle) | yes |\n| lint | golangci-lint per Go module (the repo's config, or the role's `golangci.yml`), plus `//nolint` directives, minus the role directory's `baseline` | 24 h | 20 per rule and directory | yes |\n| error-codes | pattern match over source, registry and docs files (`sh` + `jq`) | 24 h | 20 | yes |\n| trufflehog | leaked-credential scan of the current commit (`sh` + `jq` + TruffleHog) | 24 h | 1 | yes |\n| missing-tests | `go test` coverage plus exported functions with no test (`sh` + Go helper) | 24 h | 10 per package | yes |\n| trufflehog-history | leaked-credential scan of the full git history (same tools) | 7 d | 1 | no |\n| docs | undocumented exported Go identifiers and package comments (deterministic) | 3 d | 10 per package | yes |\n| docs-drift | LLM: docs that contradict the code (planned; needs the LLM scanner engine, #204) | 3 d | 10 per doc file | no |\n| architecture | LLM against ADRs and the sidecar spec repo | 3 d | 1 | no |\n| stale | built-in | 24 h | none | none |\n\n**The cve scanner** (`scanners/cve`, a `sh` script plus one `jq` program, no build step) merges osv-scanner (all ecosystems) and govulncheck (Go reachability). Its fingerprint is `osv:<advisory>:<manifest>:<package>`, where the advisory is the smallest `GHSA-` id of the vulnerability, else the smallest `CVE-` id, else the smallest id, so one vulnerability reported under `GO-`, `GHSA-` and `CVE-` ids is one finding. Severity is `critical|high|medium|low`, from the CVSS score, else the database severity, else `medium`; a vulnerability govulncheck finds unreachable is capped at `medium`. Manifests under `testdata/`, `vendor/` and `node_modules/` are skipped. It fails closed: if a Go module cann\n\n**The lint scanner** (`scanners/lint`, a `sh` script, one `jq` program and a Dockerfile that builds golangci-lint v2 with the image's Go) is Go only in v1; other ecosystems are follow-ups. It runs golangci-lint once per `go.mod` (pruning `.git`, `vendor`, `node_modules` and `testdata`) with the repo's own config, or a human-owned `golangci.yml` in the role directory when present. These flags are always forced after any config, because findings that vanish are autoclosed: `--max-issues-per-linter=0 --max-same-issues=0` (no truncation), `--new=false --new-from-rev= --new-from-merge-base= --new-from-patch=` (a repo config asking for \"new issues only\" would report nothing on a clean checkout), `\n\n**The error-codes scanner** (`scanners/error-codes`, a `sh` script plus one `jq` program, pattern match over source, registry and docs files) reads a human-owned `codes.json` in its role directory; nothing ships one, because error-code conventions are specific to each repository. Keys: `pattern` (required, a regex of at most 512 characters, wrapped so `E1001` does not match inside `XE10011`; it must not match the empty string), `registry` (required, a list of path patterns), `entry` (optional: a registry line must also match it to define a code), `docs` (optional; unset turns the undocumented check off), `sources` (optional, default every text file) and `exclude`. A path pattern ending in `/\n\n**The missing-tests scanner** (`scanners/missing-tests`, a `sh` script plus a stdlib-only Go helper) runs each Go module's own tests with `go test -coverpkg=./...` and files one finding per exported function or method that no test calls by name or that the tests never run; the group is the package, and the fingerprint does not depend on the file or line. Because it runs the repository's tests it needs the executor's container and egress to the module proxy. If a module's tests fail to build or fail, the scan fails and files nothing, so autoclose never acts on partial coverage. A human-owned `ignore` file next to `scan` skips symbols or packages, and a `no-coverage` file there skips the test \n\n**The lts scanner** (`scanners/lts`, a `sh` script plus two `jq` programs and `yq` for Helm, no build step) flags components at or near end of life using the endoflife.date v1 API (`/api/v1/products/<slug>`, one request per referenced product). A component is a product plus a release cycle. Its fingerprint is `eol:<product>:<cycle>` (for example `eol:nodejs:18`), one finding lists every referencing file, and `group` is the product. The human-owned `products.json` maps Docker image names and a few npm packages to endoflife.date slugs (the Alpine image maps to `alpine-linux`); names not in it are skipped with a stderr line. Cycles match generically: a codename's first word (`bookworm`, `jammy`\n\n**The trufflehog scanner** (`scanners/trufflehog`, a `sh` script plus one `jq` program) runs `trufflehog filesystem . --json --no-update --no-color --no-verification --no-ignore-tag --fail-on-scan-errors --concurrency=2` on the current commit. It never verifies a credential against a provider. Only the detector name, file, line and a hash of the secret leave the scan: `Raw`, `RawV2`, `Redacted`, `SecretParts`, `ExtraData` and `StructuredData` stay in pipes inside `scan`. The fingerprint is `trufflehog:` plus the first 16 hex digits of SHA-256 over `ard-trufflehog-v1`, a NUL byte and the base64 of the secret (an unsalted hash, so one value gives one issue wherever it appears). The scan fails \n\n**The tr